Buffalo Soldiers were United States Army regiments composed exclusively of Black American
soldiers, formed during the 19th century to serve on the American frontier.

12
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
On September 21, 1866, the 10th Cavalry Regiment was
formed at Fort Leavenworth, Kansas.

13
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:36,000
According to legend, during the exodus of the 1870s, more than
20,000 African Americans migrated to Kansas.

14
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:06,000
They hoped their journey would take them far away from poverty from the South.

15
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:36,000
Others escaped by being soldiers.

16
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
They served in the segregated army units and fought in
the Western Indian Wars from 1867 to 1896.

17
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:36,000
According to legend, the men were called "buffalo
soldiers" by the Apache and Cheyenne.

18
01:03:00,000 --> 01:03:06,000
The soldiers adopted the name as a sign of honor and respect.

19
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:36,000
Units of Buffalo Soldiers answered the nation's call to arms mainly in the
West, however, also in Cuba, the Philippines, Hawaii, and Mexico.

20
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
They had a huge impact on the Reconstruction era.

21
01:13:30,000 --> 01:13:36,000
Although numerous Black Union Army regiments were raised during the Civil War (referred to collectively as the United States Colored
Troops), "Buffalo Soldiers" were established by the U.S Congress as the first all-black Army regiments in peacetime.

22
